text:
alice: Heads up — Zenith POC streaming is flaky. Client reports SSE disconnects after ~6-12s and intermittent JSON.parse errors because they receive partial chunks. Meeting with their eng tomorrow; can we swarm? :eyes:

raj: On it. Can you paste a raw client-side error and whether they're using our JS SDK or a custom EventSource? Also need the request-id from the failing calls.

alice: They sent this stack: 
`SyntaxError: Unexpected end of JSON input at JSON.parse (<anonymous>)`
Client: custom EventSource polyfill in their Android webview. Using hosted Redwood API (region: us-west-2). Route: /v1/stream/chat. Request-ID: rwd-zz-1092

priya: Probable causes: 1) proxy/nginx buffering causing chunk coalescing/truncation, 2) client not handling multiline SSE framing (they may assume one line==one event), 3) intermediate load balancer (Cloudflare/FASTLY) closing idle streams.

tom: Quick checklist: 
- Confirm `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` and `Content-Type: text/event-stream` on responses.
- Check server logs around rwd-zz-1092 for partial write/errors.
- Have them log raw bytes received. If they see events split mid-json, it's a client parsing issue and we should recommend reassembly until empty-line delimiter.

raj: Requesting raw bytes snapshot from them. Meanwhile, try this workaround in the client: accumulate lines until you detect an empty line (i.e. event separator) then join and parse. Example Node pseudo: 
```
let buffer = '';
source.on('message', (chunk) => {
  buffer += chunk;
  if (buffer.endsWith('\n\n')) {
    const event = buffer.trim();
    buffer = '';
    const json = JSON.parse(event.replace(/^data:\s*/gm, ''));
    handle(json);
  }
});
```

sasha_sre: Also check their HTTP/1.1 keepalive and proxy timeouts. Nginx default `proxy_read_timeout` can close long-lived SSEs. If they're fronted by nginx, try `proxy_buffering off;` and `chunked_transfer_encoding on;`. On the Redwood side I see no immediate token rate-limit hits for that req-id.

priya: If they're on Android WebView, the built-in EventSource has bugs. Recommend: 1) switch to a streaming fetch + manual parsing, or 2) add a heartbeat ping from server (every 15s) to keep streams alive. We can enable heartbeat from our side per-route.

alice: Can we enable heartbeat for this customer's route for the next 48h? That would buy us time for their SDK fix and preserve the POC timeline. Also sales needs a one-pager on possible enterprise blockers (private deploy option).

tom: I can flip a per-route heartbeat flag in the console — will do if legal says it's okay. It's just a lightweight `:ping` SSE every 15s.

priya: I'll follow up with security on heartbeat payload (empty, no PII). For proxy suggestion, share this nginx snippet with the customer: 
```
location /v1/stream/ {
  proxy_pass http://redwood-backend;
  proxy_buffering off;
  proxy_cache off;
  proxy_http_version 1.1;
  proxy_set_header Connection '';
  chunked_transfer_encoding on;
  proxy_read_timeout 3600s;
}
```

raj: Also here's a quick cURL check they can run to emulate a stable SSE: 
`curl -N -H "Accept: text/event-stream" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  "https://api.redwood.ai/v1/stream/chat?request_id=test-sse"`

deploy-bot: deploy 2026-04-10T16:12Z: staged config change for per-route heartbeat flag (canary to 10% traffic). :rocket:

sasha_sre: I'll monitor the canary. If we see increased connection churn we roll back. Also check Cloudflare proxy timeouts — if they front the client, CF has a 100s idle timeout for some plans.

alice: Perfect — please keep me posted. If we can get the heartbeat and nginx fixes pushed, we can keep Zenith on track for the demo. Pricing note for sales: if they need a private deployment (air-gapped), that will add lead time — mention in call.

raj: Action items: 
- raj: get raw bytes from Zenith + user-agent and proxy chain (due: 2h)
- priya: share nginx snippet + heartbeat plan with Zenith (due: 1h)
- tom: enable per-route heartbeat canary (done)
- sasha_sre: watch canary metrics and logs (ongoing)

alice: Thanks team. If they can paste a raw first 2KB of the failing stream that will narrow it down quickly. :thumbsup:
